What is Linux?

Linux is an open-source operating system widely used for:

AWS EC2
Azure Virtual Machines
Google Cloud
Jenkins servers
Docker hosts
Kubernetes nodes

Interview Question:

Q: Why do most companies use Linux servers?

Answer: Linux is stable, secure, lightweight, highly customizable, and performs well for enterprise workloads. Most cloud platforms and DevOps tools run on Linux.
Step 2: Connect to Your Linux Server

If using AWS EC2:

ssh -i my-key.pem ec2-user@<public-ip>

Verify you're logged in:

whoami

Expected output:

ec2-user
Step 3: Check System Information
hostname

Shows the server name.

pwd

Shows your current directory.

date

Shows the current date and time.

uptime

Shows how long the server has been running and its load.
Step 4: Understand the Linux Directory Structure

Important directories:

Directory	Purpose
/	Root directory
/home	User home directories
/etc	Configuration files
/var	Logs and application data
/tmp	Temporary files
/usr	Installed software
/opt	Optional applications
/bin	Essential commands
/sbin	System administration commands
Step 5: Navigate Directories
pwd
ls
ls -l
ls -la
cd /etc
cd ~
cd ..
cd /

Practice these until you can move around without hesitation.
Step 6: Create Directories
mkdir devops
mkdir project

Create nested directories:

mkdir -p training/day1/linux

Check:

tree training

If tree is unavailable:

find training

Step 7: Create Files
touch file1.txt
touch file2.txt

Create multiple files:

touch app.log notes.txt test.sh
Step 8: View File Contents

Write text:

echo "Hello Linux" > file1.txt

View the file:

cat file1.txt

Also practice:

less file1.txt
head file1.txt
tail file1.txt

Step 9: Copy, Move, Rename, Delete

Copy:

cp file1.txt backup.txt

Rename:

mv backup.txt backup-old.txt

Move:

mv backup-old.txt training/

Delete:

rm file2.txt

Delete directory:

rm -r project
Step 10: Search Files
find . -name "*.txt"
find /etc -name hosts
Real-Time Scenario

A developer says:

"My application configuration file is missing."

As a Linux administrator, you might:

Find the configuration file:

find / -name application.properties

Check permissions:

ls -l application.properties

Verify the contents:

cat application.properties
Restore or replace the file if needed.
